<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <!--
    TCC usage strings. macOS shows these when the Node child first reads a
    protected location (Desktop/Documents/Downloads/removable volumes) while
    discovering or opening a vault. Without them the prompt is silent or denied.
  -->
  <key>NSDesktopFolderUsageDescription</key>
  <string>dreamcontext reads project folders on your Desktop to discover and open dreamcontext vaults.</string>
  <key>NSDocumentsFolderUsageDescription</key>
  <string>dreamcontext reads project folders in your Documents to discover and open dreamcontext vaults.</string>
  <key>NSDownloadsFolderUsageDescription</key>
  <string>dreamcontext reads project folders in your Downloads to discover and open dreamcontext vaults.</string>
  <key>NSRemovableVolumesUsageDescription</key>
  <string>dreamcontext reads project folders on removable volumes to discover and open dreamcontext vaults.</string>
  <!--
    Microphone. J.A.R.V.I.S mode is push-to-talk: the WebView calls getUserMedia({audio})
    while the mic button is held. The app is NOT sandboxed (see entitlements.plist), so this
    usage string is the ONLY missing macOS piece — no new capability and no entitlement is
    required, and without it WKWebView's getUserMedia is denied outright rather than
    prompting.

    Scope note, because the grant this unlocks is process-wide and wry does not narrow it:
    wry 0.55.1 grants WKWebView media capture unconditionally, discarding the requesting
    origin and frame. Once this prompt is satisfied once, nothing at the OS or WebKit layer
    distinguishes the composer's own mic button from markup an agent rendered into a
    sandboxed iframe. That gap is closed one layer up, by SANDBOX_ALLOW in
    dashboard/src/lib/sandboxHtml.ts — every iframe drawing authored HTML carries allow="".
  -->
  <key>NSAppleEventsUsageDescription</key>
  <string>dreamcontext pauses your music player while J.A.R.V.I.S mode reads an answer aloud, and starts it again when the answer ends.</string>
  <key>NSMicrophoneUsageDescription</key>
  <string>dreamcontext records your voice while you hold the microphone button in J.A.R.V.I.S mode, so you can speak to your agent instead of typing.</string>
</dict>
</plist>
